===========================================================================
VERDICT: UNMEASURED -- the action armed correctly, but no real loop was formed.
===========================================================================
The action DID apply, unlike T16452:
      interface port1.0.2 / loop-protection action link-down     -> accepted
      show loop-protection ->  port1.0.2  Yes  link-down  Normal
So `link-down` is compatible with fast-block where `port-disable` is not.

The ring was then closed by `no shutdown` on the AR4050S port1.0.2 and watched
for 90 s. **No loop-protection action fired, and that is expected — the loop
never actually closed.** The x230's own `port1.0.2` carries
`channel-group 1 mode active` from its restored default.cfg, and its `po1` is
notconnect, so that leg does not forward. Bringing the 4050 side up alone
leaves the third edge of the triangle dead.

THIS IS NOT A PRODUCT RESULT. It is an incomplete stimulus, recorded as such
rather than reported as "loop protection failed to fire".

TO CLOSE THIS CASE: remove `channel-group 1` from the x230's port1.0.2 (so the
leg forwards), then close the ring. That creates a genuine L2 loop on a bench
with RSTP disabled on all three devices, with loop-protection `link-down` as
the only safeguard -- which is exactly what the case is testing, but it should
be done deliberately and watched, not as a side effect.
